WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:04.480
It certainly isn’t easy to defeat the Hulk,
but if you’ve read the comics, you already

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:06.550
know that it’s not impossible.

00:00:06.550 --> 00:00:11.249
From adamantium claws, super-strength beatdowns,
and even simple bullets, the Hulk’s been

00:00:11.249 --> 00:00:15.519
taken down more times than you might think,
although he definitely always comes back.

00:00:15.519 --> 00:00:20.170
Here’s every time the Hulk has lost his
life at the hands of his enemies.

00:00:20.170 --> 00:00:21.609
Friendly Fire

00:00:21.609 --> 00:00:26.320
The Hulk's first demise came at the hands
of an anonymous soldier in a 1965 issue of

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:27.990
Tales to Astonish.

00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:32.550
After destroying one of Bruce Banner’s inventions
before it can fall into the wrong hands, the

00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:37.110
Hulk attempts to escape from The Leader's
evil lab, but is fired on by a soldier.

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:40.970
It seems he was in the middle of transforming
back into Banner at the time, because when

00:00:40.970 --> 00:00:44.540
the smoke cleared, Banner is lying on the
ground, apparently dead.

00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:48.510
Fortunately for the Hulk, Rick Jones steals
the body and brings it back to Banner's secret

00:00:48.510 --> 00:00:52.690
desert lab, hoping he can revive him by transforming
him into the Hulk.

00:00:52.690 --> 00:00:56.870
It works, but it's now Banner in control of
the Hulk's body, thanks to the bullet lodged

00:00:56.870 --> 00:01:00.940
in his skull that keeps him from turning back
into his puny alter-ego.

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:05.580
Rather than deal with a Hulk that's super-strong
and super-smart, the Hulk’s arch-nemesis,

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:09.690
the Leader, dissolves the bullet with the
most vaguely named comic book science device

00:01:09.690 --> 00:01:13.640
ever: the "Gamma-Ray Laser-Type Beam."

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:15.500
The Disease

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:19.350
Bruce Banner has found plenty of cures for
his condition over the years, but every time

00:01:19.350 --> 00:01:22.990
he does, he immediately finds a really good
reason why he shouldn’t have bothered.

00:01:22.990 --> 00:01:27.580
Such was the case for the Hulk's second death,
in Incredible Hulk #225.

00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:31.530
Having been miraculously cured, Banner was
helpless to fight off an assault on Gamma

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:32.979
Base by the Leader.

00:01:32.979 --> 00:01:37.270
While his allies tried to convince him to
re-embrace his Hulkier side, Banner had what

00:01:37.270 --> 00:01:39.010
he thought was a better idea.

00:01:39.010 --> 00:01:44.070
He strapped himself into a "Gamma Control
Harness" that he used to control a Hulk robot.

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:47.880
Unfortunately for him, the Leader defeated
the robot, and the energy feedback fatally

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:49.549
electrocuted Banner.

00:01:49.549 --> 00:01:53.840
Like Rick Jones before him, Doc Samson reasoned
the only thing to do was to blast Banner's

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:56.970
body with gamma rays and hope it turned him
back into the Hulk.

00:01:56.970 --> 00:02:00.920
It worked, but he probably regretted it when
the Hulk punched him through a wall.

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:03.290
Not a great way to say thanks, Hulk.

00:02:03.290 --> 00:02:04.770
The rematch

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:09.889
Wolverine made his first appearance all the
way back in Hulk #181, with a fight that didn’t

00:02:09.889 --> 00:02:11.200
exactly go his way.

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:16.159
14 years later, in Hulk #340, Logan returned
with a vengeance and handed the Hulk one of

00:02:16.159 --> 00:02:18.150
his most devastating defeats.

00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:22.170
The two run into each other in a snowstorm,
when the X-Men stop to save the passengers

00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:25.980
of an airline the Hulk accidentally took down
by hitting it mid-leap.

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:29.690
When Hulk finds Wolverine amidst the plane's
wreckage, he picks a fight by attacking his

00:02:29.690 --> 00:02:30.739
old rival.

00:02:30.739 --> 00:02:35.359
Wolverine initially refuses, but when he finally
cuts loose, he spears Hulk through the torso

00:02:35.359 --> 00:02:36.650
with his claws.

00:02:36.650 --> 00:02:40.620
Wolverine believes the Hulk is a goner, but
is shocked a few moments later when the gray

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:46.800
Hulk rises from the ground, clutching his
chest, saying, "Let's…try that…again."

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:47.959
Countdown

00:02:47.959 --> 00:02:50.920
The Leader seems to be involved with a lot
of these deaths.

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:53.230
You'd almost think he has a problem with the
Hulk.

00:02:53.230 --> 00:02:57.030
In a story called “Countdown,” the Hulk
experiences multiple massive heart attacks

00:02:57.030 --> 00:02:59.230
for reasons he can't figure out.

00:02:59.230 --> 00:03:02.959
After slugging it out with the Abomination,
the She-Thing, and the Leader’s various

00:03:02.959 --> 00:03:07.260
henchmen, the big-brained villain finally
tells the Hulk that he knows what's happening.

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:12.169
The Hulk has been poisoned by a villain called
Madman, and Leader wants Madman dead.

00:03:12.169 --> 00:03:16.330
After slowly weakening and shrinking over
the course of a four-part story, Hulk finally

00:03:16.330 --> 00:03:20.670
succumbs to the poison at the start of Incredible
Hulk #367.

00:03:20.670 --> 00:03:24.269
After he revives, he learns that Madman is
Leader's brother, and after defeating the

00:03:24.269 --> 00:03:28.489
bad guys, he gets the cure for the poison,
narrowly avoiding an oversized coffin once

00:03:28.489 --> 00:03:30.129
again.

00:03:30.129 --> 00:03:31.689
Future Imperfect

00:03:31.689 --> 00:03:35.329
Future Imperfect isn’t just one of the Hulk’s
many deaths, it's a two-fer.

00:03:35.329 --> 00:03:39.239
In this story, the Hulk travels to the future
and learns that one super-powered villain

00:03:39.239 --> 00:03:43.530
rules over what's left of humanity, and that
villain is a version of himself called the

00:03:43.530 --> 00:03:44.530
Maestro.

00:03:44.530 --> 00:03:48.150
He’s just as intelligent as the Bruce Banner
of the present, more vicious than the Hulk

00:03:48.150 --> 00:03:53.829
ever was, and, in spite of his advanced age,
even stronger thanks to the increased radiation

00:03:53.829 --> 00:03:54.829
in the environment.

00:03:54.829 --> 00:03:58.090
Hulk and Maestro throw down twice during Future
Imperfect.

00:03:58.090 --> 00:04:01.769
Their first battle begins in an underground
rebel base and spills out onto the streets

00:04:01.769 --> 00:04:05.720
of Dystopia before the Maestro ends it by
breaking the Hulk's neck.

00:04:05.720 --> 00:04:08.329
That's Future Imperfect's Hulk Death #1.

00:04:08.329 --> 00:04:11.980
The second comes when the Hulk uses the same
time machine that brought him to the future

00:04:11.980 --> 00:04:16.530
to send Maestro to the past, specifically,
to the exact moment the gamma bomb blast that

00:04:16.530 --> 00:04:19.099
changed Banner into the Hulk the first time.

00:04:19.099 --> 00:04:20.099
Boom.

00:04:20.099 --> 00:04:21.879
A hero unborn

00:04:21.879 --> 00:04:26.130
The Onslaught event was catastrophic for many
heroes of the Marvel Universe, but none more

00:04:26.130 --> 00:04:27.560
so than the Hulk.

00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:31.949
After sacrificing themselves to defeat Onslaught,
the Fantastic Four and the Avengers were sent

00:04:31.949 --> 00:04:36.020
to an alternate universe called “Heroes
Reborn,” where their stories were rebooted

00:04:36.020 --> 00:04:39.470
and they all lived new lives with their new,
amazing cheekbones.

00:04:39.470 --> 00:04:43.240
Meanwhile, everyone back in the regular universe
thought they were dead.

00:04:43.240 --> 00:04:45.169
For the Hulk, though, it wasn’t that simple.

00:04:45.169 --> 00:04:49.130
He was the one who delivered the blow that
shattered Onslaught's armor, and it released

00:04:49.130 --> 00:04:53.879
so much power that Bruce Banner and the Hulk
were physically separated from one another.

00:04:53.879 --> 00:04:57.789
Bruce Banner, along with the Avengers and
the Fantastic Four, was reborn in the new

00:04:57.789 --> 00:05:00.289
timeline… but the Hulk stayed where he was.

00:05:00.289 --> 00:05:04.750
In the immediate aftermath, the new, savage,
Banner-less Hulk went wild, with his insides

00:05:04.750 --> 00:05:07.240
appearing to literally rip out of his skin.

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:11.281
Eventually, an "irradiated cocoon" formed
around the him, and the following issue, a

00:05:11.281 --> 00:05:16.039
new kind of Hulk emerged from the cocoon,
one with the power and savagery of the green

00:05:16.039 --> 00:05:18.830
Hulk, but the viciousness of the gray Hulk.

00:05:18.830 --> 00:05:23.810
Years later, in Avengers #684, we learned
that the cocoon didn't just change the Hulk.

00:05:23.810 --> 00:05:25.550
It killed him.

00:05:25.550 --> 00:05:27.060
Destructible

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:31.300
After a nice, long, well-deserved vacation
from his own violent death, Bruce Banner bought

00:05:31.300 --> 00:05:34.900
the proverbial farm once again at the end
of Indestructible Hulk #20.

00:05:34.900 --> 00:05:39.300
The series ended with a mystery: An unknown
assailant shoots Banner in the back of the

00:05:39.300 --> 00:05:44.190
head on the final page, leading to the story
being relaunched with a new Hulk #1.

00:05:44.190 --> 00:05:48.819
After surgery, a really long fight with the
Abomination, and just a tiny spoonful of Iron

00:05:48.819 --> 00:05:53.349
Man’s Extremis formula, the Marvel Universe
is introduced to Doc Green: a version of the

00:05:53.349 --> 00:05:58.319
Hulk which shares Bruce Banner's intelligence,
but has a completely different personality.

00:05:58.319 --> 00:06:02.410
After launching a crusade to remove the powers
of virtually every gamma-powered monster in

00:06:02.410 --> 00:06:06.520
the Marvel Unvierse, we finally learn who
was responsible for his attempted murder.

00:06:06.520 --> 00:06:11.100
Dr. Melinda Leucenstern shot Banner, but it
was Betty Ross, his long-time love, who gave

00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:13.870
her the intelligence she needed to pull it
off.

00:06:13.870 --> 00:06:18.229
After her own adventures as the Red She-Hulk,
Ross was convinced her former husband was

00:06:18.229 --> 00:06:22.240
going to cause an extinction level event in
the near future.

00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:23.240
Heads up

00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:26.750
Here’s a Hulk death that shouldn’t count...
but apparently does.

00:06:26.750 --> 00:06:31.139
In Avengers: Ultron Forever #1, the Hulk is
part of a team of Avengers pulled from different

00:06:31.139 --> 00:06:32.509
moments in time.

00:06:32.509 --> 00:06:36.729
As soon as he enters the fray, though, he's
apparently decapitated by one of Ultron's

00:06:36.729 --> 00:06:37.729
lackeys.

00:06:37.729 --> 00:06:41.440
Then, in the issue that follows we learn that
the Hulk didn't really lose his head.

00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:42.569
He lost a mask.

00:06:42.569 --> 00:06:47.490
See, the Hulk of Ultron Forever is a very
early version of the Hulk, pulled from 1963's

00:06:47.490 --> 00:06:49.560
Incredible Hulk #6.

00:06:49.560 --> 00:06:54.580
In that strange issue, Banner transformed
into a form that had a big green Hulk body,

00:06:54.580 --> 00:06:56.430
and a little Bruce Banner head.

00:06:56.430 --> 00:06:59.020
The Hulk was forced to don a Hulk mask to
hide his identity.

00:06:59.020 --> 00:07:03.710
That’s the Hulk who shows up in Ultron Forever,
and while that shouldn’t technically count

00:07:03.710 --> 00:07:09.180
as one of his many deaths, a flashback in
Avengers #684 puts it in there with the others.

00:07:09.180 --> 00:07:15.480
It seems the Hulk actually was decapitated,
and just grew a tiny new head the next issue.

00:07:15.480 --> 00:07:17.199
The Fallen

00:07:17.199 --> 00:07:21.450
Before Civil War 2 #3, most of the heroes
of the Marvel Universe thought Bruce Banner

00:07:21.450 --> 00:07:23.099
was rid of the Hulk for good.

00:07:23.099 --> 00:07:27.370
Amadeus Cho had absorbed the gamma radiation
from Banner and became the Totally Awesome

00:07:27.370 --> 00:07:29.330
Hulk, leaving Banner completely cured.

00:07:29.330 --> 00:07:33.150
But then, thwatche Inhuman called Ulysses,
had a precognitive vision of the original

00:07:33.150 --> 00:07:37.199
Hulk on a rampage, apparently murdering most
of Marvel's heroes.

00:07:37.199 --> 00:07:41.319
When those heroes confronted Banner, they
found he was researching gamma radiation again,

00:07:41.319 --> 00:07:43.120
and they were less than happy.

00:07:43.120 --> 00:07:46.639
Before anything could be resolved, two arrows
kill Banner instantly.

00:07:46.639 --> 00:07:50.479
A flashback within a flashback revealed that
months ago, Banner had approached Hawkeye

00:07:50.479 --> 00:07:55.210
with two arrows, asking Hawkeye to use them
on him if he should ever show signs of turning

00:07:55.210 --> 00:07:56.539
into the Hulk.

00:07:56.539 --> 00:08:01.139
In the aftermath, Hawkeye claimed he saw Banner's
eyes flash with green, but the truth is left

00:08:01.139 --> 00:08:02.319
ambiguous.

00:08:02.319 --> 00:08:06.099
Whether or not Banner was about to turn into
the Hulk, it's clear that Hawkeye could never

00:08:06.099 --> 00:08:09.850
have done the deed, at least not with as much
finality as Banner wanted.

00:08:09.850 --> 00:08:13.520
Bruce Banner and the Immortal Hulk are in
the Marvel Universe for good… or for at

00:08:13.520 --> 00:08:15.909
least as long as it takes them to pick another
adjective.

